Kernir patch 0014: the 7zip reader refuses an entry name over 65,536 UTF-16 units and a names block over 512 MiB

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_7zip.c (read_Header, kName;
slurp_central_directory). Applies on top of 0004 and 0007.

read_Header mallocs the whole kName property (every entry name, UTF-16LE, NUL-terminated) and copies it in
64 KiB pieces before archive_read_next_header returns the first entry, so no limit in the caller comes in time.
In an LZMA-coded header the block costs almost nothing on disk: a 57 KB 7z of 400 names of 500 KB held
597 MB at open, a 59 KB one with one name of 200 Mi units 3 GB for 26 s, a 76 KB one with 4,097 names of 65,535
units 853 MB.

- A name over KERNIR_7Z_NAME_UNITS (65,536) UTF-16 units is refused while the block is copied, at the first
  piece that passes it (each piece is checked as it is copied; the count runs across pieces). UTF-8 takes at
  least one byte per UTF-16 unit, so such a name is over the 64 KiB of raw UTF-8 a Kernir entry name may hold,
  and nothing the caller would list is refused. The count covers the whole block, also bytes past the last
  name that upstream ignores.
- A block over KERNIR_7Z_NAMES_BYTES (512 MiB) plus 2 bytes per entry (the terminators) is refused before it is
  allocated or read. UTF-16 takes at most 2 bytes per byte of UTF-8 (1 unit per 1-3 bytes, 2 units per 4), so
  such a block holds more than the 256 MiB of raw UTF-8 names and link targets the caller lists per archive,
  and again nothing the caller would list is refused. A tighter block limit would refuse real archives: an
  ASCII backup of 1.5 M entries of 100 characters has a 300 MB block and 150 MB of UTF-8.

Both set ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT with their own message ("7-Zip entry name is longer than 65536 UTF-16
units", "7-Zip entry names take more than 512 MiB of UTF-16") and return KERNIR_7Z_REFUSED (-2) from read_Header;
slurp_central_directory returns ARCHIVE_FATAL on it without replacing the message by "Damaged 7-Zip archive".
Archives within both limits read as before.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0014-7zip-name-limits.patch

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_7zip.c
